---
// Lesson 9.5a — Datasets: Insights from Playtest Data
// expand-app: the playtests.csv dataset; aggregates, patterns, decisions.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>9.5a — Datasets: Insights from Playtest Data</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    All semester you have written playtest numbers in tables
    (<a href="6.5a.html">6.5a</a>, <a href="7.7a.html">7.7a</a>).
    Today those habits become DATA SCIENCE: one shared dataset of
    32 playtest runs, real questions, and the move from "I feel like
    the spike room is hard" to "the spike room is where 62% of deaths
    happen."
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Download the dataset.</strong> Grab
    <a href="../data/playtests.csv">playtests.csv</a> — 32 runs, 6
    sections, one row per playtester per run. Each column is a
    <em>variable</em>; each row is one <em>record</em>. The
    columns:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        <tr><td>section</td><td>Which part of the cave (1=entrance … 6=boss door)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>run_id, player_id</td><td>Which run, which tester</td></tr>
        <tr><td>deaths, deaths_by_spikes, deaths_by_enemy, deaths_by_fall</td><td>Death counts BY CAUSE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>time_seconds</td><td>Time in that section</td></tr>
        <tr><td>coins_missed</td><td>Coins the tester saw but did not get</td></tr>
        <tr><td>jumps</td><td>Jump inputs pressed (activity!)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>distance_to_boss_start</td><td>How far into section 6 they died/gave up</td></tr>
        <tr><td>completed_boss</td><td>1 = won the fight, 0 = did not</td></tr>
        <tr><td>rating (1–5), comment</td><td>Felt experience, in their words</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The exam's dataset questions hinge on knowing WHICH
            column answers WHICH question. "Which section is
            deadliest?" uses deaths; "which section FRUSTRATES?"
            needs time PLUS rating. Why can the deadliest section
            and the most-hated section be different — and what
            would a designer do differently about each?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — eyeball then compute.</strong> Open the CSV
    in any spreadsheet tool (Numbers, Google Sheets). Predict three
    things from eyeballing, THEN compute them: total deaths per
    section (sort/sum), average time per section, and the
    completion rate (completed_boss column: what fraction is 1?). A
    prediction next to a result is the whole scientific method in
    one spreadsheet.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — cross-tab questions.</strong> The interesting
    questions join columns:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Do high-jump sections have more fall deaths? (jumps vs.
            deaths_by_fall, per row)</li>
        <li>Do testers who miss more coins rate lower? (coins_missed
            vs. rating)</li>
        <li>Which section has the WORST time-per-death ratio (slow
            AND deadly = the frustration zone)?</li>
        <li>Of testers who died to the enemy, what fraction still
            beat the boss? (deaths_by_enemy &gt; 0, then
            completed_boss)</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — aggregate like the exam.</strong> AP
    pseudocode loves AVERAGE and SUM over filtered lists. Do the
    section-2 average time BY HAND as a filtered list:
    keep rows where section == 2, average time_seconds. Write it in
    exam pseudocode next to your spreadsheet answer:</p>
    <pre><code>times ← [row.time_seconds FOR row IN runs IF row.section = 2]
AVERAGE(times)</code></pre>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Data traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>One tester ran section 3 four times (four rows).
                Compare their 4 times: why is the FIRST run's time
                a different KIND of number than the fourth
                (learning effect — what does that mean for
                "average difficulty")?</li>
            <li>A row shows rating 5, deaths 7. What is that row
                telling you — and why does "hard but loved" exist
                (see <a href="7.5a.html">7.5a</a>: hard-but-fair
                vs easy-but-random)?</li>
            <li>Sort by deaths descending. The top row: what should
                you READ before you act — deaths, time, OR the
                comment? Why is the comment the only column that
                explains the number?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: One insight, one change</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write ONE insight as a full sentence with numbers:
                "Testers died 2× more in section ___ than any
                other, and their comments mention ___."</li>
            <li>Then ONE design change to YOUR cave that the insight
                implies — and nothing else. (The discipline of
                one-variable changes, now driven by data instead
                of vibes — this is the loop the whole year has
                been practicing.)</li>
            <li>Hard: which column is MISSING that would settle the
                biggest open question about your boss fight?
                (Propose it — a column is a design decision.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name variable vs record and read a CSV fluently</li>
            <li>Answer cross-column questions with spreadsheet filters/aggregates</li>
            <li>Express a filtered average in AP pseudocode</li>
            <li>Turn one dataset insight into one design change</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest dataset (CSV)</a> — the data itself</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/index.html">Unit 9: Data &amp; Systems</a> — datasets in the CED</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="9.6a.html">9.6a — Bias, Filtering &amp; Fair Data</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="9.5a" title="9.5a Datasets: Insights from Playtest Data — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
